    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold mb-4">ServerPeacock VPS Review 2026: Dutch KVM from €3.99/mo</h1>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-8">Published 2026-07-29 · Independent Dutch operator · Verified pricing + datacenter data from serverpeacock.com</p>

    <div class="bg-gray-900 border border-gray-800 rounded-xl p-6 mb-8">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mb-4">
        <span class="text-2xl font-bold">3.8/5</span>
        <span class="text-gray-500">Developer Rating</span>
      </div>
      <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
        ServerPeacock is an independent Dutch-registered VPS provider founded in 2018 that sells KVM virtualization across four datacenters (Amsterdam, Frankfurt, Dallas, New York). The pitch is straightforward and rare at the €3.99/mo entry tier: IPv6 included by default on every plan, direct-wallet crypto billing (BTC, ETH, LTC, XMR — no third-party payment processor), SEPA bank transfer for EU customers, and EUR pricing that avoids FX fees for European buyers. The differentiator vs the budget tier (<a href="https://my.racknerd.com/aff.php?aff=16299" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">RackNerd</a>, BuyVM) is not raw $/GB — ServerPeacock loses on that axis — but the bundle of EU headquarters + crypto billing + IPv6 default + renewable energy contracts, which most budget providers do not assemble in one place.
      </p>
      <p class="text-gray-400 mt-3 text-sm">Entry: €3.99/mo KVM-1 · €6.99/mo KVM-2 · €14.99/mo KVM-4 · IPv6 included on every plan</p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4">Introduction and Quick Specs</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      ServerPeacock is an independent Dutch-registered VPS provider founded in 2018 that sells KVM virtualization across four datacenters (Amsterdam, Frankfurt, Dallas, New York). The pitch is straightforward and rare at the €3.99/mo entry tier: IPv6 included by default on every plan, direct-wallet crypto billing (BTC, ETH, LTC, XMR — no third-party payment processor), SEPA bank transfer for EU customers, and EUR pricing that avoids FX fees for European buyers. The differentiator vs the budget tier (RackNerd, BuyVM) is not raw $/GB — ServerPeacock loses on that axis — but the bundle of EU headquarters + crypto billing + IPv6 default + renewable energy contracts, which most budget providers do not assemble in one place.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      ServerPeacock is not a hyperscaler substitute. There is no managed database line, no object storage, no Kubernetes, no CDN, no serverless. The product is bare KVM VPS, sized from 1 vCPU / 1 GB at €3.99/mo up to 6 vCPU / 16 GB at €29.99/mo. EU datacenters run on renewable energy contracts (per the provider's published 2024 sustainability statement); US datacenters use Equinix facilities on the mixed grid. The provider has been continuously operational since 2018 — eight years, no parent-company acquisition, no rebranding.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      This review covers the four-dimension profile most buyers care about: pricing transparency, billing surface area (the unique differentiator), datacenter footprint, and performance benchmarks. It also flags three things ServerPeacock is <em>not</em> (managed services, hyperscaler substitute, APAC presence) and names three close substitutes on this site for buyers whose requirements don't match.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4">Pricing and Plans</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      ServerPeacock's full plan matrix verified 2026-07-28 from <a href="https://serverpeacock.com" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">serverpeacock.com</a>:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <thead><tr class="border-b border-gray-800 text-gray-400"><th class="text-left py-3">Plan</th><th class="text-left py-3">vCPU</th><th class="text-left py-3">RAM</th><th class="text-left py-3">Storage</th><th class="text-left py-3">Bandwidth</th><th class="text-left py-3">Monthly</th><th class="text-left py-3">Annual (~17% off)</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-800"><td class="py-3">KVM-1</td><td class="py-3">1</td><td class="py-3">1GB</td><td class="py-3">20GB SSD</td><td class="py-3">1TB</td><td class="py-3">€3.99/mo</td><td class="py-3">€3.29/mo</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-800"><td class="py-3">KVM-2</td><td class="py-3">2</td><td class="py-3">2GB</td><td class="py-3">40GB SSD</td><td class="py-3">2TB</td><td class="py-3">€6.99/mo</td><td class="py-3">€5.79/mo</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-800"><td class="py-3">KVM-4</td><td class="py-3">4</td><td class="py-3">8GB</td><td class="py-3">100GB SSD</td><td class="py-3">5TB</td><td class="py-3">€14.99/mo</td><td class="py-3">€12.49/mo</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-800"><td class="py-3">KVM-8</td><td class="py-3">6</td><td class="py-3">16GB</td><td class="py-3">200GB SSD</td><td class="py-3">10TB</td><td class="py-3">€29.99/mo</td><td class="py-3">€24.99/mo</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      The entry €3.99/mo is in the same bracket as BuyVM's $3.50/mo flat slice — slightly higher raw price but with different defaults. Where ServerPeacock wins: IPv6 default (no upsell), no Bandwidth billing surprises on European routes, and EUR pricing without FX fees for European customers. Where ServerPeacock loses: <a href="https://my.racknerd.com/aff.php?aff=16299" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">RackNerd</a>'s $10.98/yr annual entry is ~3x cheaper than ServerPeacock's €3.99/mo monthly equivalent on per-year cost. For US-only buyers, <a href="https://my.racknerd.com/aff.php?aff=16299" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">RackNerd</a> or BuyVM are cheaper per GB.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4">What Makes ServerPeacock Different: Billing Surface Area</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      The most underappreciated feature of ServerPeacock is its <strong>billing surface area</strong>. Most VPS providers — even privacy-marketing-focused ones — route payments through a third-party processor (Stripe, PayPal, Coinbase Commerce, NOWPayments, BTCPay Server). Each of those processors stores billing metadata: card last-4, billing email, IP address of payment, FX rate at charge time, chargeback history. Combined, this metadata forms a profile that links your real-world identity (card holder) to your server account (server IP, hostname pattern, traffic).
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      ServerPeacock accepts four forms of payment, each with different metadata footprints:
    </p>
    <ul class="text-gray-300 space-y-2 mb-4 ml-6 list-disc">
      <li><strong>Credit card</strong> — standard processor (Stripe), GDPR-driven identity verification required for transactions over €15 (typical EU KYC threshold). Card-on-file; metadata footprint is the typical processor-level profile.</li>
      <li><strong>SEPA bank transfer</strong> — direct EUR transfer from an EU bank account. No third-party processor; metadata is the bank statement line. Lowest FX exposure for EU customers paying in EUR.</li>
      <li><strong>Bitcoin / Ethereum / Litecoin via direct wallet</strong> — ServerPeacock publishes a wallet address on the invoice page; buyer sends from their own wallet. No exchange / processor / custody provider in the chain. Pseudonymous by design.</li>
      <li><strong>Monero via direct wallet</strong> — same direct-wallet pattern as BTC/ETH/LTC but on Monero's privacy-by-default blockchain. Sender + receiver + amount are cryptographically obscured.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      The direct-wallet BTC/ETH/LTC/XMR pattern is what differentiates ServerPeacock from typical "we accept crypto via Coinbase Commerce" providers — the latter still route through a KYC'd custodian. ServerPeacock's model is closer to paying cash at a hosting provider's office: the payment settles, and the provider has no on-chain identity link to your fiat identity.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4">Datacenter Locations and Routing</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      ServerPeacock operates KVM nodes in four locations, all on 1Gbps uplinks with IPv6 included. EU datacenters run on renewable energy contracts per the provider's published 2024 sustainability statement (verified at serverpeacock.com/sustainability):
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <thead><tr class="border-b border-gray-800 text-gray-400"><th class="text-left py-3">Location</th><th class="text-left py-3">Region</th><th class="text-left py-3">ASN</th><th class="text-left py-3">Power</th><th class="text-left py-3">Best for</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-800"><td class="py-3">Amsterdam (NL)</td><td class="py-3">EU primary</td><td class="py-3">AS200651</td><td class="py-3">Renewable</td><td class="py-3">EU users, GDPR data residency, NL routing</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-800"><td class="py-3">Frankfurt (DE)</td><td class="py-3">EU secondary</td><td class="py-3">AS200651 (via DE-CIX)</td><td class="py-3">Renewable</td><td class="py-3">DE users, low-latency to DE-CIX peers</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-800"><td class="py-3">Dallas</td><td class="py-3">US Central</td><td class="py-3">Equinix DA1</td><td class="py-3">Mixed grid</td><td class="py-3">US Central / South users, Latin America</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-800"><td class="py-3">New York</td><td class="py-3">US East</td><td class="py-3">Equinix NY9</td><td class="py-3">Mixed grid</td><td class="py-3">US East users, financial API endpoints</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      EU datacenters are powered by renewable contracts; US datacenters run on the mixed grid (Equinix facilities). The provider publishes annual sustainability metrics on its website — a small but real differentiator vs providers who publish only marketing claims. For EU-jurisdiction workloads, Amsterdam is the primary pick (lowest latency from most of continental Europe, AS200651 has good peering). Frankfurt is the secondary for DE-specific latency.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4">Performance and Uptime</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Independent benchmarks of ServerPeacock across 2024-2026 (sourced from LowEndTalk threads, NFO Forums benchmarks, and Reddit r/selfhosted) show CPU performance in the typical mid-market range — comparable to <a href="https://my.racknerd.com/aff.php?aff=16299" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">RackNerd</a> EPYC shared, BuyVM KVM, and HostHatch EPYC at similar tier sizes. Single-thread Geekbench 6 scores cluster around 1100-1400 for KVM-1/KVM-2 tiers; multi-thread scales linearly to ~6000-7500 for KVM-8. Disk I/O on the SSD-backed nodes runs ~500MB/s sequential read, ~250MB/s sequential write (typical SATA SSD performance, not NVMe-tier).
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Uptime across the four datacenters is consistent — measured at 99.95-99.98% over rolling 12-month windows. There have been no catastrophic incidents in 2024-2026 that affected all four datacenters simultaneously; individual datacenter outages have been brief (typically under 30 minutes) and isolated to single sites. For workloads where multi-DC failover is required, ServerPeacock's four locations are insufficient — buyers needing 5+9s SLAs should look at Vultr's 32+ DC footprint or hyperscaler-grade providers.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4">What ServerPeacock Is Not</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Three things ServerPeacock is <em>not</em>, that buyers sometimes assume it is:
    </p>
    <ul class="text-gray-300 space-y-2 mb-4 ml-6 list-disc">
      <li><strong>Not a managed services provider.</strong> There is no managed tier — the customer is responsible for OS setup, security patching, backups, application deployment, and monitoring. ServerPeacock runs the hardware and the network; everything above the hypervisor is the customer's job. If you want a managed layer on top of VPS, look at Cloudways (managed WordPress/WooCommerce on AWS/DO/Vultr) or a dedicated managed provider.</li>
      <li><strong>Not a hyperscaler substitute.</strong> No managed databases, no object storage (S3-compatible), no Kubernetes, no auto-scaling groups, no CDN, no serverless functions. ServerPeacock is bare VPS only. If your workload needs managed Postgres, managed Redis, or object storage, look at Vultr (managed DBs + object storage), DigitalOcean (managed DBs + Spaces), or hyperscalers.</li>
      <li><strong>Not an APAC provider.</strong> There is no Hong Kong, Singapore, or Tokyo node. The four datacenters are split 50/50 EU/US. For Asia-Pacific coverage, look at <a href="/vps/lightnode" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">LightNode</a> (APAC-heavy footprint), Vultr (32+ DCs incl. Tokyo/Singapore), or Asia-region hyperscalers.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4">Who Should Choose ServerPeacock</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      ServerPeacock is the right pick for a narrow but durable set of buyer profiles:
    </p>
    <ul class="text-gray-300 space-y-2 mb-4 ml-6 list-disc">
      <li><strong>EU-based indie developers and small teams</strong> who want an EU-headquartered provider for GDPR / data-residency reasons, and don't want to pay hyperscaler rates.</li>
      <li><strong>Privacy-respecting small businesses</strong> that have already decided they don't want card-on-file with a hyperscaler or US-based reseller, and want direct-wallet crypto + EU registration.</li>
      <li><strong>IPv6-first deployments</strong> where IPv4 is a fallback rather than primary — ServerPeacock's IPv6 default on every plan matches this pattern.</li>
      <li><strong>SEPA-paying EU customers</strong> who want to avoid FX fees on EUR payments — ServerPeacock's EUR pricing + SEPA direct transfer is the cleanest path.</li>
      <li><strong>Backup VPS for transatlantic workloads</strong> needing both EU and US in one provider — ServerPeacock's four-datacenter footprint covers Amsterdam + Frankfurt + Dallas + New York in a single relationship.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      ServerPeacock is <em>not</em> the right pick for buyers who need managed services, GPU instances, APAC presence, hyperscaler-grade SLAs, or absolute cheapest $/GB. Those profiles have better answers in the comparison table below.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4">Three Close Substitutes</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Three providers on this site that occupy adjacent positions to ServerPeacock:
    </p>
    <ol class="text-gray-300 space-y-3 mb-4 ml-6 list-decimal">
      <li><strong><a href="/vps/racknerd" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">RackNerd</a></strong> — $10.98/yr annual entry (cheaper than ServerPeacock's €3.99/mo monthly on annual basis), 8 datacenters including LA, NYC, Chicago, Dallas, Amsterdam. Closer to the budget tier; less emphasis on EUHQ + crypto billing + IPv6 default. <a href="/blog/racknerd-deep-review-2025" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">RackNerd review</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>BuyVM</strong> — $3.50/mo flat slice entry, unmetered bandwidth on Las Vegas / Luxembourg / New York / Frankfurt. Cheaper than ServerPeacock on raw $/GB but no SEPA, no IPv6 default, no Dutch registration. BuyVM review.</li>
      <li><strong><a href="/vps/hosthatch" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">HostHatch</a></strong> — $4/mo entry with 1GB RAM + AMD EPYC, 14 regions including EU + US + APAC. Better APAC coverage than ServerPeacock, similar price, less emphasis on EUHQ + crypto billing. <a href="/blog/hosthatch-vps-review-2026" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">HostHatch review</a>.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4">Final Verdict</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      ServerPeacock occupies a useful but narrow niche in the EU mid-market VPS segment: independent Dutch operator, IPv6 default, direct-wallet crypto billing, four transatlantic datacenters, EUR pricing. The pitch will resonate strongly with EU-based indie developers, privacy-respecting small businesses, and buyers who specifically want a non-hyperscaler EU headquarters for GDPR / data-residency reasons. It will not resonate with buyers chasing the absolute cheapest $/GB, buyers needing hyperscaler-grade managed services, or buyers in APAC.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      The biggest differentiator is the <strong>billing surface area</strong> — direct-wallet Monero on a Dutch-registered provider is a meaningful configuration for buyers who have already decided they don't want card-on-file with a hyperscaler. Combine that with IPv6 default and SEPA for EU customers, and ServerPeacock becomes the closest "EU budget EU-native" pick on this site. The provider is small enough that you should still keep off-site backups and verify the EU datacenters' actual location vs claimed location at signup — but the operational pattern is well-documented in the LowEndTalk / Reddit / NFO Forums threads over 2018-2026.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      For buyers deciding between ServerPeacock and its closest substitutes on this site: pick ServerPeacock if you specifically need EU headquarters + crypto billing + IPv6 default, even at a 20-40% premium over RackNerd. Pick <a href="https://my.racknerd.com/aff.php?aff=16299" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">RackNerd</a> or BuyVM if raw $/GB is the only decision variable. Pick Vultr or DigitalOcean if you need managed databases, object storage, or APAC presence.
    </p>

